```c
void copy_data(int *restrict dest, const int *restrict src, size_t n) {
    for (size_t i = 0; i < n; ++i) {
        dest[i] = src[i];
    }
}
```
Adding `restrict` tells the compiler that `dest` and `src` point to non-overlapping memory regions. This allows the compiler to optimize by, for example, loading multiple `src` values into registers before storing them to `dest`, without needing to worry that a store to `dest` might inadvertently modify a `src` value that was yet to be read.
